1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,995
Lesson 0.3.

2
00:00:02,375 --> 00:00:03,826
Probability refresher.

3
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:08,453
A thermodynamic computer does
not return exact numbers:

4
00:00:08,453 --> 00:00:11,702
it returns samples from a
probability distribution.

5
00:00:12,082 --> 00:00:15,910
To understand what it computes,
how noisy its answer is and how

6
00:00:15,910 --> 00:00:19,373
much information it contains,
we need a precise language.

7
00:00:19,753 --> 00:00:23,854
In this lesson we review the
pieces we will use all semester:

8
00:00:23,854 --> 00:00:27,954
Bernoulli, categorical and
Gaussian variables, their moments,

9
00:00:27,954 --> 00:00:31,450
Shannon entropy and the
Kullback–Leibler divergence.

10
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:37,968
Let's start with a biased coin that
comes up one with probability p = 0.7.

11
00:00:38,348 --> 00:00:39,914
It is a Bernoulli variable.

12
00:00:40,294 --> 00:00:43,531
Let's flip it many times and
track the fraction of ones.

13
00:00:43,911 --> 00:00:47,468
At first the curve
swings a lot, but as the

14
00:00:47,468 --> 00:00:50,777
number of flips grows it approaches 0.7.

15
00:00:51,157 --> 00:00:53,747
This is the law of large
numbers: the average of

16
00:00:53,747 --> 00:00:56,175
many samples converges
to the expected value.

17
00:00:56,555 --> 00:01:00,419
If instead of two outcomes there
are K, like a die, we have a

18
00:01:00,419 --> 00:01:05,043
categorical variable, described by a
probability vector that sums to one.

19
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:08,505
Now let's add up many Bernoulli variables.

20
00:01:08,885 --> 00:01:12,788
If we add twenty flips and repeat
the experiment thousands of

21
00:01:12,788 --> 00:01:16,052
times, the histogram of
the sum takes a bell shape.

22
00:01:16,432 --> 00:01:19,997
The green curve is a
Gaussian with the same

23
00:01:19,997 --> 00:01:23,810
mean, n p, and the same
variance, n p (1 − p).

24
00:01:24,190 --> 00:01:27,313
This is the central limit
theorem: sums of many

25
00:01:27,313 --> 00:01:30,303
independent contributions
tend to a Gaussian.

26
00:01:30,683 --> 00:01:33,096
That is why thermal noise, the sum of

27
00:01:33,096 --> 00:01:36,096
countless microscopic
collisions, is Gaussian.

28
00:01:36,476 --> 00:01:46,318
The density is p(x) =
exp(−(x−μ)²/2σ²)/√(2πσ²).

29
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:51,900
In several dimensions, the point
cloud of a standard Gaussian is

30
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:55,794
round: each coordinate is
independent with variance one.

31
00:01:56,174 --> 00:02:00,585
Let's apply a linear transformation
A to each point and add a vector b.

32
00:02:00,965 --> 00:02:03,475
The cloud stretches, rotates and shifts.

33
00:02:03,855 --> 00:02:08,772
The new mean is b, and the
new covariance matrix is A Aᵀ.

34
00:02:09,152 --> 00:02:12,519
The drawn ellipse encloses
the region where most of the

35
00:02:12,519 --> 00:02:15,886
probability lives: its axes
are the eigenvectors of the

36
00:02:15,886 --> 00:02:19,865
covariance, and its lengths, the
square roots of the eigenvalues.

37
00:02:20,245 --> 00:02:23,079
This transformation
rule is the basis of the

38
00:02:23,079 --> 00:02:26,428
thermodynamic linear solver
we will see in unit six.

39
00:02:27,550 --> 00:02:29,825
Moments summarize a distribution.

40
00:02:30,205 --> 00:02:33,063
The first moment is the
mean, the center of mass.

41
00:02:33,443 --> 00:02:37,147
The second central moment is the
variance, which measures the width.

42
00:02:37,527 --> 00:02:41,112
For a Gaussian, the mean and the
covariance determine everything.

43
00:02:41,492 --> 00:02:45,512
This will have a deep consequence:
a physical system relaxing

44
00:02:45,512 --> 00:02:49,335
toward a Gaussian stores the
answer to a linear problem in

45
00:02:49,335 --> 00:02:52,696
its mean, and the inverse
matrix in its covariance.

46
00:02:53,817 --> 00:02:55,867
How much information
does an outcome carry?

47
00:02:56,247 --> 00:03:01,000
Shannon proposed measuring it by surprise,
minus the log of its probability.

48
00:03:01,380 --> 00:03:05,043
A certain event is no surprise;
a rare one is very surprising.

49
00:03:05,423 --> 00:03:11,459
Entropy is the average
surprise: H = −Σ p log p.

50
00:03:11,839 --> 00:03:15,424
For a coin, the curve is
zero if the outcome is

51
00:03:15,424 --> 00:03:19,314
certain and peaks at one
bit when the coin is fair.

52
00:03:19,694 --> 00:03:22,974
For a Gaussian, entropy
grows with the log of the

53
00:03:22,974 --> 00:03:26,120
standard deviation: wider
means more uncertain.

54
00:03:27,250 --> 00:03:31,215
To compare two distributions
we use the Kullback–Leibler

55
00:03:31,215 --> 00:03:35,958
divergence: the average, under p,
of the log of the ratio p over q.

56
00:03:36,338 --> 00:03:39,061
It measures how many
extra bits we pay if we

57
00:03:39,061 --> 00:03:41,970
encode data from p using
a code designed for q.

58
00:03:42,350 --> 00:03:44,861
It is always greater than
or equal to zero, and

59
00:03:44,861 --> 00:03:47,159
is zero only if the
distributions coincide.

60
00:03:47,539 --> 00:03:49,994
But it is not a distance:
it is not symmetric.

61
00:03:50,374 --> 00:03:53,214
Watch the two numbers
as the orange Gaussian

62
00:03:53,214 --> 00:03:55,924
moves and widens: they change differently.

63
00:03:57,050 --> 00:03:58,316
Let's do two calculations.

64
00:03:58,696 --> 00:04:04,586
The entropy of a coin with
p = 0.9 is minus 0.9 times

65
00:04:04,586 --> 00:04:09,920
log base two of 0.9, minus
0.1 times log of 0.1.

66
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:13,470
The result is 0.47 bits.

67
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:16,939
Second: the divergence between
a standard normal and a

68
00:04:16,939 --> 00:04:19,684
normal with mean one and
standard deviation two.

69
00:04:20,064 --> 00:04:27,230
The closed form gives log of two, plus a
quarter, minus a half: about 0.44 nats.

70
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:30,570
These pieces appear throughout the course.

71
00:04:30,950 --> 00:04:33,162
The pbit is a Bernoulli variable whose

72
00:04:33,162 --> 00:04:35,665
probability is controlled
by a local field.

73
00:04:36,045 --> 00:04:40,919
The final state of the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck
process is a multivariate Gaussian.

74
00:04:41,299 --> 00:04:43,692
Entropy sets the Landauer cost.

75
00:04:44,072 --> 00:04:47,381
And the KL divergence
appears in Boltzmann machine

76
00:04:47,381 --> 00:04:51,220
learning, in diffusion models
and in the natural gradient.

77
00:04:52,350 --> 00:04:55,018
In the next lesson we will see symmetric

78
00:04:55,018 --> 00:04:59,219
positive-definite matrices, which
shape the covariance ellipse.

79
00:04:59,599 --> 00:05:04,742
Then we will study Markov chains,
where distributions evolve in time.

80
00:05:05,122 --> 00:05:09,815
In unit one we will connect Shannon
entropy with Gibbs entropy, and in unit

81
00:05:09,815 --> 00:05:14,195
two we will learn to put error bars
on averages of correlated samples.

82
00:05:15,317 --> 00:05:22,137
Frequent mistakes: the covariance of
A y plus b is A Aᵀ, not A squared.

83
00:05:22,517 --> 00:05:26,667
The KL divergence is not a distance,
because it is not symmetric.

84
00:05:27,047 --> 00:05:30,198
Watch the units: with
log base two we measure

85
00:05:30,198 --> 00:05:32,859
in bits, with the natural log in nats.

86
00:05:33,239 --> 00:05:37,423
And remember that the differential
entropy of a continuous density

87
00:05:37,423 --> 00:05:41,164
can be negative, unlike the
entropy of a discrete variable.

88
00:05:42,283 --> 00:05:43,116
In summary:

89
00:05:43,496 --> 00:05:47,658
Bernoulli, categorical and Gaussian
are the basic variables of the course.

90
00:05:48,038 --> 00:05:51,047
A linear transformation takes a standard

91
00:05:51,047 --> 00:05:54,657
Gaussian to one with mean
b and covariance A Aᵀ.

92
00:05:55,037 --> 00:05:59,437
Entropy measures average surprise, and
the KL divergence measures how much

93
00:05:59,437 --> 00:06:03,183
one distribution differs from
another, without being symmetric.

94
00:06:04,317 --> 00:06:08,774
To practice: find the mean
and covariance of A y plus b.

95
00:06:09,154 --> 00:06:13,391
Compute the entropy of a Bernoulli
and of a d-dimensional Gaussian.

96
00:06:13,771 --> 00:06:16,649
Compare the two KL
divergences between a normal

97
00:06:16,649 --> 00:06:19,405
with variance one and
one with variance four.

98
00:06:19,785 --> 00:06:22,637
And compute how many flips
you need to estimate a

99
00:06:22,637 --> 00:06:25,606
probability with a standard
error of one hundredth.
